WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:12.812
I'm Sara. I'm an art conservator

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.114
and the director of conservation

00:00:14.114 --> 00:00:16.816
at Center Art Studio in New York City.

00:00:16.816 --> 00:00:19.686
Today, I'm going to walk
you through how we restore

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:22.756
a 16th-century sculpture of St. George.

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:27.127
This sculpture is owned by a
collector in New York City,

00:00:27.127 --> 00:00:29.396
and he brought the sculpture to us

00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:32.999
when it was damaged in
transit from Barcelona.

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:35.635
The first thing we do

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:38.304
when we get an object
into the studio to restore

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:40.473
is perform an initial inspection.

00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:42.275
So, we want to understand

00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:45.111
what is the current
condition of the object.

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:46.246
And once we understand that,

00:00:46.246 --> 00:00:49.449
then we are able to start
formulating a treatment plan.

00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:54.421
This is a sculpture of St.
George slaying a dragon.

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:58.625
The lore is that the villagers
had to sacrifice somebody

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:00.226
to this dragon every year.

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:03.630
And then one year it became
the princess of the town

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.298
that was going to be sacrificed.

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:07.700
And so St. George the soldier came in,

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.903
slayed the dragon, saved the princess,

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:12.539
and, you know, has been
revered and is a saint.

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:15.508
This is a depiction of St. George,

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:18.812
but it actually has the face and the hair

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:20.747
of King Ferdinand II.

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:23.750
Clearly, it's very well carved.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:26.453
It's polychrome, gilded.

00:01:26.453 --> 00:01:30.690
It is a significant piece that
would've been well funded.

00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:34.060
After we come up with a treatment plan,

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.328
the first thing that we do

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:39.099
is separate the figure, dragon, and base,

00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:40.433
and we are going to clean

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:42.335
each one of those components separately.

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:48.608
It has a very, very heavy,
glossy varnish on top of it,

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:52.011
which just is unnecessary.

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:55.014
It comes across as heavy handed.

00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:58.184
We can tell that there's dirt

00:01:58.184 --> 00:02:01.688
sitting under the top varnish layer.

00:02:01.688 --> 00:02:05.692
So that means that whoever
last varnished this piece

00:02:05.692 --> 00:02:09.996
and worked on it didn't
fully clean it properly.

00:02:09.996 --> 00:02:14.400
They put new coats of material over dirt

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.070
and bad restorations.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.772
We start out with solvent gels,

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:22.375
and we apply the gel to the surface,

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:26.346
and the gel holds the solvent

00:02:26.346 --> 00:02:28.314
against the surface.

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:32.552
So it allows it to penetrate
a little bit better.

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:35.455
When the gel's on the surface,
we come in with a swab

00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:37.657
and kind of agitate
the gel to turn it over

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:42.061
to make sure that, you
know, fresh, clean gel

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:45.532
is making contact with that
area that we're cleaning.

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:48.168
The most difficult component to clean

00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:50.036
and get the varnish off of is the base,

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:54.407
just because it is so
thick and heavy and waxy.

00:02:54.407 --> 00:02:59.412
So that takes a bit more
time and care to remove.

00:02:59.846 --> 00:03:03.550
We look with a UV
flashlight at the surface.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:06.152
And if we see green reflecting back,

00:03:06.152 --> 00:03:07.687
that indicates to us that there is

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:10.924
a yellowed varnish or
coating on that surface.

00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:15.295
It's typical to have us actually
clean with a flashlight,

00:03:15.295 --> 00:03:18.765
so we can see where we're
removing those green areas.

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:23.469
The face and the hands
are, they're very dirty,

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:25.071
and with a proper cleaning,

00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:26.539
they're going to really transform

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:30.343
from kind of a dull, murky color

00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:32.345
to a bright skin color

00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:34.781
that really is dramatically different

00:03:34.781 --> 00:03:37.584
than how it looked pre-cleaning.

00:03:37.584 --> 00:03:42.288
When we remove that gel that's
cleaned off all those layers,

00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:46.259
we see, in that cleaned area,
we see old crackle lines.

00:03:46.259 --> 00:03:48.061
And that crackle is desirable

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:51.331
because it just indicates
the age of the piece.

00:03:51.331 --> 00:03:52.232
And you want to see,

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:55.034
you'd expect an object from the 1500s

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:58.605
to have lots of variation and age to it.

00:03:58.605 --> 00:04:01.140
So once you remove those layers,

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.276
you start to see the
complexity of the surface,

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:07.480
and it just takes on a whole
new substantial feeling.

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:11.751
When art is in transit,

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:15.588
that's when it's really at
most risk of incurring damage.

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:18.992
And unfortunately, that's just
what happened in this case.

00:04:18.992 --> 00:04:20.360
It couldn't support itself.

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.862
I mean, it's meant to be
a freestanding object,

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:25.698
and it could not
vertically support itself.

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:29.402
To do the structural
repairs on this figure,

00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:31.571
we have to remove

00:04:31.571 --> 00:04:34.707
the iron rods that are going through

00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:36.643
the feet of St. George

00:04:36.643 --> 00:04:39.746
and into the dragon.

00:04:39.746 --> 00:04:43.182
Those are held into the sculpture

00:04:43.182 --> 00:04:47.153
with some kind of bonding glue,

00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:49.956
epoxy resin. We're not
exactly sure what that is.

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:51.891
So we need to break that bond

00:04:51.891 --> 00:04:54.560
in order to get those rods out.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:59.132
This is obviously a very
tricky, delicate procedure,

00:04:59.132 --> 00:05:01.901
because we are going to
have to remove those rods,

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:04.737
but we really don't want to
cause any further damage.

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:08.041
So we test a variety of methods.

00:05:08.041 --> 00:05:11.311
So we will chip off a bit of that compound

00:05:11.311 --> 00:05:16.316
and put it in a vial of water or solvents

00:05:16.316 --> 00:05:18.284
and do a test to see,

00:05:18.284 --> 00:05:21.721
we can see if that little chip dissolves.

00:05:22.555 --> 00:05:23.923
So we were able to determine that

00:05:23.923 --> 00:05:28.861
it would soften in water,
but it didn't dissolve it.

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:32.865
So we could use water to help us.

00:05:32.865 --> 00:05:35.702
But we're still going
to have to use drills

00:05:35.702 --> 00:05:38.538
and more mechanical approaches.

00:05:38.538 --> 00:05:42.241
So we sort of break away that old epoxy

00:05:42.241 --> 00:05:44.911
that's holding the rods
into the sculpture,

00:05:44.911 --> 00:05:49.716
and we use vise grips and clamps as we go.

00:05:49.716 --> 00:05:53.252
We add a bit of water while
we're wiggling the rod

00:05:53.252 --> 00:05:55.788
to break that bond.

00:05:55.788 --> 00:05:59.058
Ultimately, we switch out
those tools for a drill.

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:00.626
At first, we use a regular drill bit

00:06:00.626 --> 00:06:02.595
to try to break it up a bit,

00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:05.098
and then we have to switch
out to an extra-long drill bit

00:06:05.098 --> 00:06:07.500
because these recesses
are actually very deep.

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:10.803
So we use an extra-long drill bit

00:06:10.803 --> 00:06:12.305
to drill out that compound,

00:06:12.305 --> 00:06:15.875
and that ultimately allows
us to remove the rod.

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:20.246
Then we have to dig out the holes,

00:06:20.246 --> 00:06:22.682
the recesses where the rods were,

00:06:22.682 --> 00:06:27.687
in order to remove all of the
epoxy and just start fresh.

00:06:27.854 --> 00:06:30.723
So we use a drill with a Forstner bit

00:06:30.723 --> 00:06:34.394
to enlarge the holes to
about the diameter of an inch

00:06:34.394 --> 00:06:35.762
in order to have enough space

00:06:35.762 --> 00:06:38.197
to take the new stainless-steel rods

00:06:38.197 --> 00:06:41.300
and the epoxy that we're going to put in.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:44.470
The iron rods that we took
out were in very deep,

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:45.805
deeper than they needed to be.

00:06:45.805 --> 00:06:48.341
So we are going to plug those holes

00:06:48.341 --> 00:06:50.977
with little pieces of wooden dowel

00:06:50.977 --> 00:06:54.814
that will use fish glue
to glue into the recess

00:06:54.814 --> 00:06:58.117
just to make the hole
a little bit shallower.

00:06:58.117 --> 00:07:01.287
It works really well.
It's a natural material.

00:07:01.287 --> 00:07:04.957
It binds really well. It dries clear.

00:07:04.957 --> 00:07:07.960
It's literally just made
from condensed fish.

00:07:10.263 --> 00:07:12.632
In order to prep the sculpture

00:07:12.632 --> 00:07:15.334
to receive the rods,

00:07:15.334 --> 00:07:18.371
we had to repair the broken foot.

00:07:18.371 --> 00:07:20.907
So we used conservation epoxies

00:07:20.907 --> 00:07:24.510
to reattach the broken
components of the foot together

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:27.213
to make it one piece again.

00:07:27.213 --> 00:07:29.382
And then once it's solid,

00:07:29.382 --> 00:07:32.785
it's in a position to
then take the new rods.

00:07:34.086 --> 00:07:38.591
We test-fit them into St.
George and the dragon,

00:07:38.591 --> 00:07:41.427
and we hold up the
sculpture so we can align

00:07:41.427 --> 00:07:44.964
where everything is going to fit together.

00:07:44.964 --> 00:07:47.133
And then we'll mark on the dragon

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:50.503
where the stainless-steel rods meet it.

00:07:50.503 --> 00:07:55.441
We use our very old, but
very handy, drill press

00:07:55.441 --> 00:07:59.712
to drill out those recesses
that we've marked on the dragon.

00:07:59.712 --> 00:08:01.481
So where we've marked those circles,

00:08:01.481 --> 00:08:03.149
that's where we're going to drill.

00:08:03.149 --> 00:08:04.984
And that will just create the recess

00:08:04.984 --> 00:08:09.121
that will allow the rods
to sit onto the dragon.

00:08:10.089 --> 00:08:14.694
Then we attach the figure to the dragon.

00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:20.500
At that point, St. George and
the dragon become one object,

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:23.135
so they are bonded together.

00:08:23.135 --> 00:08:26.205
Then we have to hold it in position.

00:08:26.205 --> 00:08:28.107
So we'll tie up the sculpture

00:08:28.107 --> 00:08:31.544
and make sure it stays
in a stable position,

00:08:31.544 --> 00:08:34.680
ideally overnight, while
the epoxy fully cures

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.182
and becomes really hard.

00:08:36.182 --> 00:08:38.050
The next morning when we
come back to the studio,

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:41.320
we can untie it, and it will hold its own,

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:44.390
and it will be stable
and able to stand up.

00:08:45.625 --> 00:08:47.159
After the structural repairs are done,

00:08:47.159 --> 00:08:51.597
we apply a final coat of
shellac to the entire object.

00:08:51.597 --> 00:08:54.300
We use a thin coat of shellac.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.270
We can always add more and build it up,

00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:01.340
but we always start with a thin
coat and see how that looks.

00:09:02.241 --> 00:09:04.443
Typically, you don't
need a very heavy coat.

00:09:04.443 --> 00:09:07.446
It's unnecessary, and
it just presents better

00:09:07.446 --> 00:09:11.717
if there's less sitting
on top of the surface.

00:09:11.717 --> 00:09:13.119
This was a significant project.

00:09:13.119 --> 00:09:14.687
There was a lot of work that went into it.

00:09:14.687 --> 00:09:17.089
So there were weeks of active work.

00:09:18.324 --> 00:09:21.093
I'd say about five or
six of our conservators

00:09:21.093 --> 00:09:24.497
had their hand in restoring St. George.

00:09:26.399 --> 00:09:28.200
I'm very satisfied with this restoration.

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:32.004
I think work we did was not insignificant.

00:09:32.004 --> 00:09:34.540
It was a major undertaking.

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:38.244
It was, I think, a very
successful project,

00:09:38.244 --> 00:09:39.812
and we all feel very accomplished

00:09:39.812 --> 00:09:41.614
that we were able to restore it

00:09:41.614 --> 00:09:44.550
and get it back to this point.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.218
So we're very happy with it.

00:09:48.955 --> 00:09:52.191
It's just beautiful, and
it has a very strong,

00:09:52.191 --> 00:09:54.961
powerful presence to it
when you see it in person.

